# Cloth — a rectangular patch of simulated fabric, driven by newton's SolverVBD.
#
# ⚠ A Cloth ONLY simulates under a WorldInfo.newtonSolver value that builds a
# SolverVBD, and there are exactly two:
#
#   newtonSolver "mujoco+vbd"   THE DEFAULT CHOICE. SolverMuJoCo keeps every
#                               rigid body and joint; SolverVBD owns only the
#                               particles; SolverCoupledProxy exchanges them
#                               over one Model. Keeps MuJoCo's joint features
#                               and friction cone, keeps every mj_model-backed
#                               device, and is the only path offering per-body
#                               control of what the fabric can see
#                               (Solid.newtonClothCoupling).
#
#   newtonSolver "vbd"          ONE SolverVBD owns rigid bodies AND particles.
#                               Reach for it when a rigid body must GRIP the
#                               fabric: only here are the pad and the particle
#                               relaxed in the same sweep, where a coupled
#                               proxy always pinches against a one-step-stale
#                               jaw. The price is the whole MuJoCo feature set
#                               (no mj_model, no raycast devices, no welds, no
#                               TouchSensor, no contact readback, and five
#                               joint features newton drops in silence). Read
#                               that field's own note before choosing it.
#
# In any world declaring a purely rigid value the node still parses, still
# RENDERS at its authored rest pose (so the patch can be placed and framed in
# the editor), but registers no particles and never moves — and says so, once,
# with a warning naming the field. That warning is the only thing separating
# "misconfigured" from "the physics is broken", so it is worth reading rather
# than a bare silent freeze.
#
# Geometry. The patch is a grid of dimX x dimY CELLS — so (dimX + 1) x (dimY + 1)
# particles — spanning dimX*cellX by dimY*cellY metres in its own local frame,
# lying flat in local Z = 0 with its (0, 0) corner at `translation`. `rotation`
# turns the whole patch, so a vertical curtain is a rotated patch, not a
# different node.
#
# Pinning. fixLeft / fixRight are the low / high ends of the patch's LOCAL X
# axis, fixBottom / fixTop the low / high ends of its LOCAL Y axis. None of them
# is a world up/down claim — a patch rotated to hang vertically still pins along
# its own axes. A pinned edge's particles get zero mass and stop integrating, so
# the patch hangs from them.
#
# Self-contact (do the fabric's own particles collide with each other?) is a
# WORLD setting, not a per-Cloth one -- it is a SolverVBD parameter and there is
# one solver. WorldInfo.newtonClothSelfContact: -1 unset (the runtime default,
# ON), 0 off, 1 on. ⚠ DRAPING wants ON and GRASPING wants OFF, and the gap is
# 24x on a pinch (-22.11 mm tracking on vs -0.92 mm off): fabric gathered
# between two pads self-collides and pushes itself back out of the jaws. Read
# that field's note before authoring a world where something GRIPS this node.
#
# Rendering is engine-owned (as for GranularGroup): the simulated particle
# positions are streamed into a WREN dynamic mesh every frame, so there is no
# Shape / IndexedFaceSet child to author and none is accepted.
#
# Normals are recomputed every frame from the simulated positions and are never
# stale. They are ANGLE-weighted (each face normal weighted by the interior angle
# it subtends at the vertex), not the customary area-weighted — because a solver
# changes triangle AREAS every step, so an area-weighted normal is a function of
# the local strain and its shading swims across exactly the folds you want to
# look at. There is nothing to author here; it is recorded because it is the
# reason cloth shading is steady rather than shimmering.
#
# ⚠ MESH RESOLUTION IS AN AUTHORING DECISION AND IT SHOWS. Smooth per-vertex
# normals interpolate ACROSS a triangle, so they can only be as smooth as the
# triangle is small relative to the fold. Measured on the shipped 616-vertex
# T-shirt, the garment's own surface is genuinely crumpled at roughly 4 cm — the
# scale of its own triangles — and the result reads as hard-edged shading shards
# rather than fabric. Prefer the 2394-vertex / 4674-triangle `tshirt_hi.obj`
# beside it for anything the camera gets close to; there is no resolution ceiling
# in this node (the index buffer is 32-bit and the per-frame cost is linear in
# triangles), and the nearest limit anywhere is WREN's 65,535-vertex cutoff for
# shadow CASTING, ~27x above the hi-res shirt.
#
# FRAMING. A Cloth has no Shape/geometry children, so the harness supervisor's
# bounds walk — which works by reading geometry fields — finds nothing to measure
# and `GET /scene/tree?bounds=1` reports `bounds: null`, which is what makes
# `POST /scene/frame {"def": "SHEET"}` fail with SUBJECT_BOUNDS_NOT_FOUND. The
# node itself now measures the world-space box around its CURRENT, deformed
# surface (OmCloth::worldBounds / worldCenterAndRadius), so the engine-side half
# of that answer exists; until the supervisor is taught to ask for it, a cloth
# world's Viewpoint still has to be authored by hand.

Cloth {
  field SFVec3f    translation     0 0 1     # world position of the patch's local (0, 0) corner (MESH: of the mesh's own origin)
  field SFRotation rotation        0 0 1 0   # world orientation of the patch (axis x/y/z + angle in rad)
  # ⚠ `url` SELECTS A SECOND, MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE MODE: MESH CLOTH (a garment).
  #
  # Leave it empty and this is the rectangular patch the rest of this file
  # describes. Set it to a mesh file OmniSim can load (assimp: obj, stl, dae,
  # ply, glTF, ... -- NOT usd) and the geometry comes entirely from that file:
  # one particle per mesh vertex, one FEM triangle per face, and the bending
  # elements DERIVED from mesh adjacency, so a garment needs no edge list.
  #
  # What is IGNORED in mesh mode, each with its own parse warning if you set it:
  # dimX, dimY, cellX, cellY (the mesh supplies the geometry), `mass` (use
  # `density` -- the unit is different, see below), and all four fix* pins.
  #
  # ⚠ THERE IS NO PINNING FOR A MESH CLOTH. fixLeft/Right/Bottom/Top name the
  # edges of a rectangular lattice and a garment has none; newton's mesh entry
  # point offers no equivalent and this node does not invent one. A mesh cloth
  # is FREE and must be held up by rigid geometry -- drape it over something.
  # (fixTop defaults to TRUE, so a mesh Cloth warns about it unless you clear
  # it.)
  #
  # The mesh is CLEANED at load -- duplicate vertices welded, degenerate and
  # duplicate triangles dropped, orphan vertices dropped -- and the counts are
  # reported as a warning when any of it was needed. That is not tidiness: all
  # three are silent in newton and damaging in the world. An ORPHAN VERTEX keeps
  # mass 0.0, which newton reads as infinite inverse mass, so it becomes an
  # immovable nail holding the fabric in mid-air; an UNWELDED DUPLICATE (what a
  # UV-split seam exports as) leaves two unrelated particles, so the garment has
  # no stretch and no bending across that seam and splits open there.
  #
  # A ready-made T-shirt ships at
  # projects/samples/demos/worlds/physics/meshes/tshirt.obj (616 vertices, 1157
  # triangles, 0.664 m^2), generated from newton's own unisex_shirt.usd by
  # scripts/dev/usd_to_cloth_obj.py. ⚠ A HIGHER-RESOLUTION VERSION SITS BESIDE
  # IT — tshirt_hi.obj, 2394 vertices / 4674 triangles — and it is the one to
  # reach for whenever the camera is close enough to see the fabric: at 616
  # vertices the triangles are ~4 cm, the same scale as the garment's own
  # creases, and smooth shading across them reads as hard-edged shards instead
  # of cloth. The cost is linear (4x the particles, 4x the triangles) and there
  # is no ceiling in the node.
  #
  # ⚠ NEITHER SHIPPED ASSET HAS SPLIT VERTICES: both carry exactly one position
  # and one UV per vertex (616/616 and 2394/2394), so the position weld below is
  # a no-op on them and there is no seam to shade across. An asset exported WITH
  # UV splits is welded anyway — the weld is mandatory for the physics — so the
  # renderer's index buffer always addresses welded positions and a seam can
  # never become a permanent lighting crease.
  field MFString   url             []        # mesh file for MESH mode; empty = the grid patch below
  field SFFloat    density         0.3       # MESH only: mass per unit AREA (kg/m^2), NOT per particle. 0.3 = light cotton jersey; the shipped T-shirt lands at 0.199 kg
  field SFFloat    meshScale       1         # MESH only: multiplies the mesh's own coordinates BEFORE rotation and translation (upstream's order). Use it for an asset authored in cm (0.01)
  # MESH only. Pins every vertex within this many metres of the mesh's MAXIMUM
  # LOCAL Z, measured in the mesh's own frame before meshScale/rotation/
  # translation. 0 = unpinned. On a garment authored as worn, that band is the
  # shoulders and neck, so a shirt hangs as it would on a hanger.
  #
  # ⚠ IN PRACTICE A MESH CLOTH NEEDS THIS, and the reason is measured rather
  # than stylistic: UNPINNED CLOTH RESTING ON A STATIC BODY SINKS THROUGH IT on
  # the current solver. A 616-particle shirt dropped on a static box passed
  # through and kept falling; so did a matched 196-particle GRID sheet from the
  # same height onto the same box, faster (z = -1.20 by 8.4 s against the
  # shirt's -0.42 at 5.4 s). The defect is the existing cloth-vs-statics path,
  # not mesh authoring -- the grid patch above has it too, and the shipped grid
  # drape world only looks fine because its sheet is PINNED and merely brushes
  # the box. Until that is fixed, hang a garment; do not rest it on something.
  field SFFloat    pinTopBand      0         # MESH only: pin vertices within this distance (m) of max local Z. 0 = unpinned
  field SFInt32    dimX            20        # cells along local X (>= 1). Particles along X = dimX + 1
  field SFInt32    dimY            20        # cells along local Y (>= 1). Particles along Y = dimY + 1
  field SFFloat    cellX           0.05      # cell size along local X (m); total width = dimX * cellX
  field SFFloat    cellY           0.05      # cell size along local Y (m); total depth = dimY * cellY
  field SFFloat    mass            0.001     # PER-PARTICLE mass (kg). Default 21x21 particles => ~0.44 kg total
  # Stiffnesses. 0 = "leave it to the runtime" on all four, the same convention
  # the WorldInfo newton* fields use. What the runtime then does differs per
  # field: triKe and edgeKe have flat defaults (1e5 and 0.01), while triKa and
  # triKd are DERIVED from triKe (ka = ke, kd = 1e-2 * ke) using the ratios
  # newton's own coupled-solver example uses. ⚠ Consequence of 0 meaning unset:
  # a genuinely zero stiffness cannot be authored here — set a tiny value.
  field SFFloat    particleRadius  0         # per-particle collision thickness (m). 0 => 0.01
  field SFFloat    triKe           0         # triangle stretch stiffness. 0 => 1e5. Raise for a stiffer, less stretchy fabric
  field SFFloat    triKa           0         # triangle area-preservation stiffness. 0 => derived from triKe
  field SFFloat    triKd           0         # triangle damping. 0 => derived from triKe. Raise to kill jitter/ringing
  field SFFloat    edgeKe          0         # bending stiffness across edges. 0 => 0.01. Raise for canvas, lower for silk
  field SFBool     fixLeft         FALSE     # pin the local -X edge
  field SFBool     fixRight        FALSE     # pin the local +X edge
  field SFBool     fixBottom       FALSE     # pin the local -Y edge
  field SFBool     fixTop          TRUE      # pin the local +Y edge, so the default patch hangs and droops instead of dropping flat
  field SFColor    diffuseColor    0.8 0.2 0.2   # engine-owned phong diffuse colour, used ONLY when `appearance` is NULL
  # ⚠ THE FIELD THAT MAKES A Cloth RENDER LIKE THE REST OF THE SCENE.
  #
  # Leave it NULL and the node keeps its original engine-owned Phong material
  # driven by `diffuseColor` above -- unchanged, so no existing world moves.
  # Set it to the SAME `PBRAppearance` (or legacy `Appearance`) node any Shape
  # takes and the fabric goes through the identical WREN material path every
  # Solid uses: baseColorMap, roughnessMap, metalnessMap, normalMap,
  # occlusionMap, emissiveColorMap, textureTransform, and the sky's image-based
  # lighting. `diffuseColor` is then IGNORED (the appearance carries the colour).
  #
  # ⚠ WHY THIS IS NOT COSMETIC. The canonical world recipe declares
  # `ambientIntensity 0` (projects/objects/lights/protos/OmniSimSun.proto), so
  # ALL fill light in an OmniSim world is image-based and reaches a surface only
  # through a PBR material. A Phong Cloth therefore renders EXACTLY (0,0,0) on
  # every square millimetre the sun does not hit directly, which on a draped
  # garment is most of it. That -- not mesh resolution -- is the single biggest
  # reason a Phong cloth looks unlike everything around it.
  #
  # ⚠ BACK FACES. Fabric is a zero-thickness surface and half of a draped one is
  # seen from behind, so face culling is off and a back face is currently shaded
  # with the FRONT face's normal. That is a measured choice, not an oversight:
  # the textbook normal flip made a pinned horizontal sheet 39.9% pure black seen
  # from below (from 0.0%), because a correctly-unlit back face has no ambient to
  # fall into under `ambientIntensity 0`. The real fix is two-sided lighting in
  # the PBR shader with an ambient term behind it, gated per material; a
  # PBRAppearance Cloth asks for that bit as soon as the renderer offers it, and
  # a legacy Appearance (Phong) keeps today's behaviour either way.
  #
  # UVs. A grid patch is parameterised over its own (dimX, dimY) lattice, so a
  # texture tiles across it directly. A MESH cloth uses the UV set the asset
  # carries; only when the file has none does it fall back to a planar XY
  # projection of the rest pose, which is a placeholder and will look like one
  # on anything but a flat sheet. ⚠ Positions are welded before simulating (see
  # `url`), so a vertex split ONLY by a UV seam collapses to one particle and
  # keeps the FIRST of its UVs -- one texel column of smear along each seam, in
  # exchange for a garment that does not tear open there. Author seams where a
  # real garment has them and it does not show.
  field SFNode     appearance      NULL          # Appearance | PBRAppearance. NULL => the legacy phong `diffuseColor` above
  # HISTORY (WREN era -- WREN was deleted 2026-08-23, 976b9449d; under wgpu, the
  # only renderer, this field is a plain shadow-caster toggle). This defaulted to
  # FALSE for a while because TRUE crashed the engine with an access violation
  # (0xC0000005) on the GL driver's own worker thread. That was FIXED in WREN's
  # DynamicMesh.cpp::updateGlShadow (since deleted) and the default is TRUE
  # again; the mechanism is recorded here because the symptom pointed nowhere
  # near the cause.
  #
  # WrDynamicMesh keeps a SECOND vertex array for the shadow pass (mShadowCoords,
  # two vec4 per vertex), and addCoord() only mirrors into it once the mesh has
  # a ShadowVolumeCaster attached — which happens in Renderable, i.e. only once
  # the Renderable exists. OmCloth fills the mesh BEFORE it builds its
  # Renderable, so mShadowCoords stayed empty, its "dirty" flag stayed false, and
  # the shadow VBO was never given a data store at all. The silhouette pass still
  # computed real indices from the index buffer, so glDrawElements ran against a
  # buffer with no storage and the driver dereferenced NULL + index * 16 —
  # measured on the drape world as a faulting read at 0x23e0 == 574 * 16, index
  # 574 of 578 = 2 x 289 particles, in JIT'd driver code (hence "no loaded
  # module") with no OmniSim frame on the stack. updateGlShadow() now re-derives
  # mShadowCoords whenever it does not match the coords, so the ordering is a
  # supported one for any node, not just this one.
  #
  # ⚠ NOTE FOR ANYONE READING AN OLDER COMMENT: the material's STENCIL programs
  # are NOT what crashed. OmCloth sets them (as OmMuscle does) so the patch takes
  # part in the per-light stencil pass — without them it renders through
  # Scene::renderStencilWithoutProgram, unlit by the shadow-aware path, but it
  # does not fault.
  field SFBool     castShadows     TRUE      # whether the patch casts a shadow (silhouette is recomputed per light per frame)
}
